repl_list — implementation plan (pilot)
=======================================

status: planned (2026-07-09)

why this tool first: it is the minimal full-chain builtin — zero
arguments, read-only, backed by an fname the ML dispatcher already
implements (MCP_Repl.thy:130) and the build-time self-test already
exercises. implementing it forces every shared seam (builtin table,
tools/list merge, tools/call dispatch, MCP_Session.ir, yxml stripping)
to exist without any per-tool complexity on top, and the spec's testing
section already names its cases at all four layers ("dispatch repls []",
"session.ir(\"repls\", [])").

advertised metadata (the builtin table row)
-------------------------------------------

name          repl_list        (builtin: always the bare name)
description   "List all open REPL proof sessions. Each entry shows the
              REPL id, its step count (plus stale steps, if any), the
              origin it was initialized from, and whether it is
              currently busy executing an operation. REPLs are created
              with repl_init or repl_init_from_source and discarded
              with repl_remove."
              (source: mcp_server.py's "List all REPL sessions.",
              enriched with what Ir.repls actually prints — ir.ML:710.)
inputSchema   {"type": "object"}
              (zero-arg; the schema is mandatory anyway per "advertised
              tool metadata". no properties, no required.)
annotations   {"readOnlyHint": true, "idempotentHint": true,
               "openWorldHint": false}
              spec refinement: the spec's annotation blanket puts all
              of repl_* on the mutating side. repl_list (like
              repl_show/repl_state/repl_text, planned later) only
              reads the repl table — Ir.repls is a Synchronized.value
              read plus formatting, no state change. honest per-tool
              annotations override the coarse blanket; fold this back
              into the spec's "advertised tool metadata" bullet.
outputSchema  none (phase 2 policy: free-form captured prover output).

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp client edge   tools/call name "repl_list", arguments {}
  scala builtin row handler: backend.ir("repls", Nil)
  bridge            protocol command MCP.ir [id, "repls", yxml_args]
                    with yxml_args = encoding of the empty pair list
  ml dispatcher     fname "repls", no args -> Ir.repls ()
  engine            ir.ML:710 — folds the repl table, one "    <id>
                    (N steps[, M stale], from <origin>[, busy ...])"
                    line per repl via out = writeln

the exposed name (repl_list) and the fname (repls) differ deliberately:
exposed names follow the spec's tool table (repl_ prefix groups the
lifecycle for the model); fnames mirror mcp_server.py/Ir verbatim.
the builtin table row is the single place that records the mapping.

implementation steps
--------------------

1. [infra, shared] MCP_Session.ir(fname, args): clone of ml_run
   (mcp/src/mcp_session.scala:154) — send protocol command "MCP.ir"
   with a fresh id and the yxml-encoded pair list, park a promise in
   an id-keyed table, resolve it from a protocol-handler function for
   "MCP.ir_result", strip markup with XML.content(YXML.parse_body(_)).
   this is the first unchecked item in the spec's implementation order
   and is shared by all wave-1 tools.
2. [infra, shared] [done 2026-07-09] named-args encoding, both sides: scala encodes
   List[(String, String)] via XML.Encode (list of string pairs); the
   ML dispatcher's signature changes from `string list` (positional,
   current MCP_Repl.thy:105) to the decoded pair list with per-fname
   key lookup (missing required key / unparsable value -> the existing
   bad()/error path). repl_list itself only needs "empty args" to
   survive the migration, but the migration happens here because the
   dispatcher signature is one commit, not per tool.
3. [infra, shared] builtin tool table + merge: a scala
   Builtin_Tool(name, description, schema_json, annotations_json,
   handler) list; tools/list = builtins ++ ml registry through the
   pure exposure-name function; tools/call resolves the name through
   the same function and runs the handler, converting the client's
   json arguments object to the (key, value) pair list.
4. [this tool] add the repl_list row with the metadata above and
   handler `_ => backend.ir("repls", Nil)`; backend ir errors map to
   isError tool results (not json-rpc errors).
5. tests (below) at all four layers; rebuild sessions per
   .claude/rules/theories-rebuild.md before trusting ML results.

assumptions and how to test them
--------------------------------

infrastructure assumptions (I1..I5 — stated here once, later plans
reference them by number):

I1  MCP_Session.ir exists and resolves the promise by id on
    MCP.ir_result, off the ML protocol loop (async).
    test [bridge, mcp_test -b]: session.ir("repls", Nil) returns
    status ok; the spec's async case (slow call then fast call, fast
    reply first) guards the non-blocking property that a synchronous
    regression would silently break.
    test [scala unit, mcp_test]: MCP.ir_result payload decode against
    a canned message (clone of the decode_tools test); Fake_Backend
    grows fake ir() so no prover is needed.

I2  the argument encoding is the named yxml pair list on both sides,
    and the empty argument object encodes to something the dispatcher
    accepts for a zero-arg fname.
    test [scala unit]: encode {} -> empty pair list; encode a map with
    newline/symbol values -> round-trips byte-clean.
    test [ml unit, MCP-HOL-Tests]: dispatch "repls" with empty args ->
    ok; dispatch "repls" with a spurious key -> status error (closed
    arity), not an uncaught exception.

I3  the builtin table is merged into tools/list and tools/call routes
    to the handler, with builtins keeping the bare name on collision.
    test [scala unit]: tools/list against Fake_Backend contains
    repl_list with description and inputSchema {"type": "object"};
    tools/call "repl_list" {} reaches backend.ir with ("repls", Nil)
    (assert on what the fake saw); register a fake ml tool named
    repl_list -> builtin keeps the bare name, ml tool lists qualified;
    "repl_list" matches ^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{1,64}$ via the pure
    exposure-name function test.

I4  yxml stripping yields the plain listing text.
    test [scala unit]: XML.content(YXML.parse_body(_)) of a canned
    markup-bearing result equals the plain text.

I5  output routing: Ir.repls reports via writeln (out), which the
    Private_Output wrappers capture into the request's buffer, and
    concurrent requests don't cross.
    test [ml unit]: the spec's output-routing cases (writeln in a
    registered group lands only in that buffer; output outside any
    group falls through). already indirectly covered by the build-time
    self-test asserting on captured output.

tool-specific assumptions:

T1  the dispatcher maps "repls" to Ir.repls () and rejects arguments.
    holds today for the positional signature (MCP_Repl.thy:130);
    must survive the I2 migration.
    test [ml unit]: dispatch "repls" [] -> ok with a well-formed
    listing (the spec's named case).

T2  Ir.repls () on an empty repl table returns ok with an empty (or
    header-only) listing — not an error. the tool must be callable
    before any repl exists, because the model will probe it first.
    test [ml unit]: on fresh state (after MCP_Repl.reset and no init),
    dispatch "repls" [] -> status ok; output contains no repl ids.

T3  a created repl appears in the listing with its id and step count,
    and a removed repl disappears — the listing reflects the live
    Synchronized table, no caching anywhere in the chain.
    test [ml unit]: init "T" [Main]; dispatch "repls" -> output
    contains "T" and "0 steps"; step once -> "1 steps"; remove "T" ->
    "T" gone.
    test [e2e, test_agentic.py]: tools/call repl_list before and after
    repl_init shows the same transition through the full stack.

T4  read-only honesty (backs the readOnlyHint refinement): calling
    repl_list changes nothing — repeating it yields the same output,
    and an interleaved repl is unaffected.
    test [ml unit]: dispatch "repls" twice around an active repl ->
    identical output (modulo the busy elapsed-time field, which only
    appears while an operation is in flight — assert on the id/steps/
    origin fields, not the elapsed seconds); the repl still steps
    afterwards.

T5  the busy annotation is transient: a repl executing a step shows
    "busy [...]" and reverts after completion. relevant because the
    tool's description promises it.
    test [bridge]: fire a slow step (sleeping isar text) on repl A,
    then session.ir("repls", Nil) concurrently -> listing shows A busy;
    after join, listing shows A idle. (bridge layer because it needs
    real concurrency through the async command; also doubles as an I1
    async witness.)

